{=============================================================================
  Free convection from plates and a sphere: Nusselt-number correlations
  CoolSolve Library model CSL-0093 (function library, kind = function)
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Five correlations for natural convection from an isothermal surface: one for a
  vertical plate (Churchill & Chu 1975), three for a horizontal plate (McAdams
  1954; VDI Heat Atlas 2010 with the buoyancy-enhanced forms of Stewartson 1958
  and the lower-surface form of Schlünder 1987; Rohsenow, Hartnett & Cho 1998)
  and one for a sphere (Churchill, as presented in Schlünder 1987).

  All of them return the Nusselt number of the surface; the surface
  heat-transfer coefficient follows from h = Nu*k/L_c and is left to the caller.
  The fluid properties enter only through the dimensionless groups Pr and Gr
  (Ra = Pr*Gr), which are arguments: no property function is called inside a
  function, so the calling model passes Pr and Gr itself.

  The three horizontal-plate correlations distinguish the buoyancy-assisted case
  (hot plate, heat transfer from its upper surface) from the non-assisted one
  (cold plate, heat transfer from its lower surface); in EES the Python boolean
  `buoyancy` becomes the argument `buoyancy`: 1 = buoyancy assisted (the ht
  default True), 0 = not assisted. Their non-smooth regime switches use IF.

  The demonstration program at the end calls every function once with the input
  set of the ht doctest of the corresponding correlation ("A" cases), once with
  a second input set in the laminar range of the horizontal-plate correlations
  ("B" cases) and once in the turbulent range ("C" cases); every branch of the
  regime switches is exercised. The ht values of all 26 outputs are tabulated in
  the README (verification table).

  Two selectors of the ht family are only dispatchers
  (Nu_free_vertical_plate(_methods), Nu_free_horizontal_plate(_methods): they
  select a method by name) and are not translated; an EES caller selects the
  function directly.

  Inputs : Pr [-], Gr [-] (on the plate height for a vertical plate, on the
           characteristic length of the plate for a horizontal plate, on the
           sphere diameter for a sphere), buoyancy [-]
  Outputs: Nu [-] (one FUNCTION per correlation)
  Units  : SI - dimensionless numbers
  Source : ht (Caleb Bell), ht/conv_free_immersed.py, version 1.2.0, commit
           85e0ee6, MIT - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht ; correlations by the
           authors cited in each function block - see README.md
  Status : VERIFIED against the doctests and the values of ht 1.2.0
           (README.md, "Verification")
=============================================================================}

FUNCTION Nu_vertical_plate_Churchill(Pr, Gr)
{NU_VERTICAL_PLATE_CHURCHILL - Nusselt number, natural convection from an
isothermal vertical plate, Churchill & Chu (1975). The equation gives a smooth
transition from the laminar to the turbulent regime.

  Nu_L = [0.825 + 0.387*Ra_L^(1/6)/{1+(0.492/Pr)^(9/16)}^(8/27)]^2

  Ra = Pr*Gr,  Gr is taken on the plate height [m]

Checked with the original source by the author of ht. Outside the range, no
warning is given.
Data: Example 9.2 of Bergman, Lavine, Incropera & DeWitt (see the reference).
Validity: all Rayleigh numbers (the equation interpolates the laminar and the
turbulent branch of a vertical plate); as in the original, it can be applied to
a vertical cylinder as well, subject to D/L >= 35/Gr_L^(1/4).
Original: Churchill, Stuart W., and Humbert H. S. Chu, "Correlating Equations
  for Laminar and Turbulent Free Convection from a Vertical Plate", International
  Journal of Heat and Mass Transfer 18(11): 1323-1329, 1 November 1975,
  doi:10.1016/0017-9310(75)90243-4; also presented in Bergman, Theodore L.,
  Adrienne S. Lavine, Frank P. Incropera and David P. DeWitt, Introduction to
  Heat Transfer, 6E, Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2011 (Example 9.2).
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_free_immersed.py,
  Nu_vertical_plate_Churchill, MIT License, C. Bell -
  https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  Ra = Pr*Gr
  Nu_vertical_plate_Churchill = (0.825 + 0.387*Ra^(1/6)/(1 + (0.492/Pr)^(9/16))^(8/27))^2
END

FUNCTION Nu_horizontal_plate_McAdams(Pr, Gr, buoyancy)
{NU_HORIZONTAL_PLATE_MCADAMS - Nusselt number, natural convection from an
isothermal horizontal plate, McAdams (1954). Four forms are used, two for the
laminar and two for the turbulent regime, because the buoyancy of the plate
changes the flow: if the plate is hot, buoyancy lifts the fluid off the plate and
enhances the free convection, whereas if the plate is cold the cold fluid above
it settles on it and decreases the free convection.

  Nu = 0.54*Ra^(1/4)     Ra <= 1E7,  buoyancy assisted (hot plate)
  Nu = 0.15*Ra^(1/3)     Ra >  1E7,  buoyancy assisted
  Nu = 0.27*Ra^(1/4)     Ra <= 1E10, not buoyancy assisted (cold plate)
  Nu = 0.15*Ra^(1/3)     Ra >  1E10, not buoyancy assisted

  Ra = Pr*Gr,  Gr is taken on the characteristic length of the plate [m]

buoyancy = 1 selects the buoyancy-assisted forms (the ht default, True), 0 the
non-assisted ones. The switches between the forms are not smooth (as in ht).
Outside the range, no warning is given.
Data: air; Gr of the ht doctests is based on the characteristic length.
Validity: the four Rayleigh-number branches above; McAdams gives no range.
Original: McAdams, William Henry, Heat Transmission, 3E, Malabar, Fla:
  Krieger Pub Co, 1985 (first edition 1942).
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_free_immersed.py,
  Nu_horizontal_plate_McAdams, MIT License, C. Bell -
  https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  Ra = Pr*Gr
  IF buoyancy = 1 THEN
    IF Ra <= 1E7 THEN
      sel = 0
    ELSE
      sel = 1
    ENDIF
  ELSE
    IF Ra <= 1E10 THEN
      sel = 2
    ELSE
      sel = 3
    ENDIF
  ENDIF
  IF sel = 1 THEN
    Nu_horizontal_plate_McAdams = 0.15*Ra^(1/3)
  ELSE
    IF sel = 3 THEN
      Nu_horizontal_plate_McAdams = 0.15*Ra^(1/3)
    ELSE
      IF sel = 2 THEN
        Nu_horizontal_plate_McAdams = 0.27*Ra^0.25
      ELSE
        Nu_horizontal_plate_McAdams = 0.54*Ra^0.25
      ENDIF
    ENDIF
  ENDIF
END

FUNCTION Nu_horizontal_plate_VDI(Pr, Gr, buoyancy)
{NU_HORIZONTAL_PLATE_VDI - Nusselt number, natural convection from an isothermal
horizontal plate, VDI Heat Atlas (2010). Three forms are used, one for the
laminar and one for the turbulent regime at the upper surface of the plate and
one at its lower surface; the lower-surface form is recommended for the laminar
flow regime. As in McAdams, the buoyancy of the plate changes the flow: if the
plate is hot, buoyancy lifts the fluid off the plate and enhances the free
convection, whereas if the plate is cold the cold fluid above it settles on it and
decreases the free convection.

  Nu = 0.766*(Ra*f2)^0.2      Ra*f2 < 7E4,  buoyancy assisted (hot plate)
  Nu = 0.15*(Ra*f2)^(1/3)     Ra*f2 >= 7E4, buoyancy assisted
  Nu = 0.6*(Ra*f1)^0.2                          not assisted (cold plate)

  f2 = {1 + (0.322/Pr)^0.55}^(20/11)      f1 = {1 + (0.492/Pr)^(9/16)}^(-16/9)
  Ra = Pr*Gr,  Gr is taken on the characteristic length of the plate [m]

The characteristic length suggested for use is, with a and b being the length and
the width of the plate, L = a*b/{2*(a+b)}; the form of the Grashof number
argument is that of the caller.
buoyancy = 1 selects the upper-surface (hot plate) forms (the ht default, True),
0 the lower-surface one. The switch between the two upper-surface forms is not
smooth (as in ht). Outside the range, no warning is given.
Validity: the three branches above; the lower-surface form is recommended in the
laminar regime.
Original: Gesellschaft, V. D. I., ed., VDI Heat Atlas, 2nd ed., 2010 edition,
  Berlin; New York: Springer, 2010. The buoyancy-enhanced (upper surface) cases
  are from Stewartson, Keith, "On the Free Convection from a Horizontal Plate",
  Zeitschrift fuer Angewandte Mathematik und Physik ZAMP 9(3): 276-282,
  1 September 1958, https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02033031; the lower-surface
  correlation is said to be from Schluender, Ernst U, and International Center
  for Heat and Mass Transfer, Heat Exchanger Design Handbook, Washington:
  Hemisphere Pub. Corp., 1987, although the equations there are not quite the
  same and do not include the Prandtl number correction.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_free_immersed.py,
  Nu_horizontal_plate_VDI, MIT License, C. Bell -
  https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  Ra = Pr*Gr
  IF buoyancy = 1 THEN
    f2 = (1 + (0.322/Pr)^0.55)^(20/11)
    IF Ra*f2 < 7E4 THEN
      Nu_horizontal_plate_VDI = 0.766*(Ra*f2)^0.2
    ELSE
      Nu_horizontal_plate_VDI = 0.15*(Ra*f2)^(1/3)
    ENDIF
  ELSE
    f1 = (1 + (0.492/Pr)^(9/16))^(-16/9)
    Nu_horizontal_plate_VDI = 0.6*(Ra*f1)^0.2
  ENDIF
END

FUNCTION Nu_horizontal_plate_Rohsenow(Pr, Gr, buoyancy)
{NU_HORIZONTAL_PLATE_ROHSENOW - Nusselt number, natural convection from an
isothermal horizontal plate, Rohsenow, Hartnett & Cho (1998). Three forms are
used, a laminar and a turbulent form at the upper surface of the plate, combined
into one tenth-power mean, and one at its lower surface; the lower-surface form
is recommended for the laminar flow regime. As in McAdams, the buoyancy of the
plate changes the flow: if the plate is hot, buoyancy lifts the fluid off the
plate and enhances the free convection, whereas if the plate is cold the cold
fluid above it settles on it and decreases the free convection.

  Nu = {Nu_l^10 + Nu_t^10}^(1/10)            buoyancy assisted (hot plate)

  Nu_l = 1.4/LN{1 + 1.4/Nu_T}
  Nu_T = 0.835*Cl*Ra^(1/4)            (average Cl)
  Cl   = 0.0972 - (0.0157 + 0.462*C_tV)*t1 + (0.615*C_tV - 0.0548 - 6E-6*Pr)*t2
  Nu_t = C_tU*Ra^(1/3)
  C_tU = 0.14*(1 + 0.01707*Pr)/(1 + 0.01*Pr)
  C_tV = 0.13*Pr^0.22/{1 + 0.61*Pr^0.81}^0.42

  Nu = 2.5/LN{1 + 2.5/Nu_T}                   not assisted (cold plate)
  Nu_T = 0.527*Ra^0.2/{1 + (1.9/Pr)^0.9}^(2/9)

  Ra = Pr*Gr,  Gr is taken on the characteristic length of the plate [m]

t1 = Ah/A is the heated to non-heated area ratio and t2 = Lf*P/A the vertical
distance between the lowest and the highest point of the body (P: perimeter, A:
area); both are set to 1 and 0 respectively, as in ht for a plain plate, so the
t2 term of Cl vanishes. No friction/C term is used in the lower-surface form.
The characteristic length suggested for use is, with a and b being the length
and the width of the plate, L = a*b/{2*(a+b)}.
buoyancy = 1 selects the upper-surface forms (the ht default, True), 0 the
lower-surface one. The switch between the two faces is not smooth (as in ht).
Outside the range, no warning is given.
Validity: no range is given in the source; the lower-surface form is recommended
in the laminar regime.
Original: Rohsenow, Warren, James Hartnett and Young Cho, Handbook of Heat
  Transfer, 3E, New York: McGraw-Hill, 1998.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_free_immersed.py,
  Nu_horizontal_plate_Rohsenow, MIT License, C. Bell -
  https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  Ra = Pr*Gr
  IF buoyancy = 1 THEN
    C_tU = 0.14*(1 + 0.01707*Pr)/(1 + 0.01*Pr)
    C_tV = 0.13*Pr^0.22/(1 + 0.61*Pr^0.81)^0.42
    t1 = 1    "heated to non-heated area ratio Ah/A [-], as in ht"
    t2 = 0    "vertical distance between the lowest and the highest point Lf*P/A [-], as in ht"
    Cl = 0.0972 - (0.0157 + 0.462*C_tV)*t1 + (0.615*C_tV - 0.0548 - 6E-6*Pr)*t2
    Nu_T = 0.835*Cl*Ra^0.25    "average Cl"
    Nu_l = 1.4/LN(1 + 1.4/Nu_T)
    Nu_t = C_tU*Ra^(1/3)
    m = 10
    Nu_horizontal_plate_Rohsenow = (Nu_l^m + Nu_t^m)^(1/m)
  ELSE
    Nu_T = 0.527*Ra^0.2/(1 + (1.9/Pr)^0.9)^(2/9)    "no friction/C term"
    Nu_horizontal_plate_Rohsenow = 2.5/LN(1 + 2.5/Nu_T)
  ENDIF
END

FUNCTION Nu_sphere_Churchill(Pr, Gr)
{NU_SPHERE_CHURCHILL - Nusselt number, natural convection from an isothermal
sphere, Churchill, as presented in Schluender (1987). The equation gives a
smooth transition from the laminar to the turbulent regime.

  Nu_D = 2 + 0.589*Ra_D^(1/4)/{1+(0.469/Pr)^(9/16)}^(4/9)
           *{1 + 7.44E-8*Ra_D/{1+(0.469/Pr)^(9/16)}^(16/9)}^(1/12)

  Ra = Pr*Gr,  Gr is taken on the sphere diameter [m]

Checked with the original source by the author of ht. Outside the range, no
warning is given.
Validity: good for Ra < 1E13; the limit of Nu is 2 at low Grashof numbers.
Original: Schluender, Ernst U, and International Center for Heat and Mass
  Transfer, Heat Exchanger Design Handbook, Washington: Hemisphere Pub. Corp.,
  1987 (correlation of Churchill, S. W.).
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_free_immersed.py,
  Nu_sphere_Churchill, MIT License, C. Bell -
  https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  Ra = Pr*Gr
  Nu_sphere_Churchill = 2 + 0.589*Ra^0.25/(1 + (0.469/Pr)^(9/16))^(4/9)*(1 + 7.44E-8*Ra/(1 + (0.469/Pr)^(9/16))^(16/9))^(1/12)
END

"!Demonstration program: every correlation is called at least once"
"It is ignored by the future $INCLUDE library:free_conv_plates_and_sphere (CS-FEAT-IMPORT)."
"Case A: the input set of the ht doctest of each correlation (published values)."
"  _cold suffix: buoyancy = 0 (cold plate, heat transfer at the lower surface)."
"  _lowPr suffix: the second ht doctest of Nu_horizontal_plate_McAdams (Pr = 0.01)."
"Case B: second input set, in the laminar range of the horizontal-plate"
"  correlations (Pr = 0.7, Gr = 1E4, Ra = 7E3)."
"Case C: third input set, in the turbulent range (Pr = 0.71, Gr = 2E10, Ra = 1.42E10)."
"All 26 outputs of the three sets are tabulated against ht in the README."

"Case A - input sets (the ht doctests)"
Pr_A_plate = 0.69      "air, ht doctest of Nu_vertical_plate_Churchill with Gr = 2.63E9 [-]"
Gr_A_plate = 2.63E9
Pr_A_horiz = 5.54      "ht doctests of the three horizontal-plate correlations with Gr = 3.21E8 [-]"
Gr_A_horiz = 3.21E8
Pr_A_lowPr = 0.01      "second ht doctest set of Nu_horizontal_plate_McAdams [-]"
Gr_A_lowPr = 3.21E8
Pr_A_sphere = 0.7      "ht doctest of Nu_sphere_Churchill with Gr = 1E7 [-]"
Gr_A_sphere = 1E7

"Nu_vertical_plate_Churchill"
Nu_vertical_plate_Churchill_A = Nu_vertical_plate_Churchill(Pr_A_plate, Gr_A_plate)
"Nu_horizontal_plate_McAdams"
Nu_horizontal_plate_McAdams_A = Nu_horizontal_plate_McAdams(Pr_A_horiz, Gr_A_horiz, 1)
Nu_horizontal_plate_McAdams_A_cold = Nu_horizontal_plate_McAdams(Pr_A_horiz, Gr_A_horiz, 0)
Nu_horizontal_plate_McAdams_A_lowPr = Nu_horizontal_plate_McAdams(Pr_A_lowPr, Gr_A_lowPr, 1)
Nu_horizontal_plate_McAdams_A_lowPr_cold = Nu_horizontal_plate_McAdams(Pr_A_lowPr, Gr_A_lowPr, 0)
"Nu_horizontal_plate_VDI"
Nu_horizontal_plate_VDI_A = Nu_horizontal_plate_VDI(Pr_A_horiz, Gr_A_horiz, 1)
Nu_horizontal_plate_VDI_A_cold = Nu_horizontal_plate_VDI(Pr_A_horiz, Gr_A_horiz, 0)
"Nu_horizontal_plate_Rohsenow"
Nu_horizontal_plate_Rohsenow_A = Nu_horizontal_plate_Rohsenow(Pr_A_horiz, Gr_A_horiz, 1)
Nu_horizontal_plate_Rohsenow_A_cold = Nu_horizontal_plate_Rohsenow(Pr_A_horiz, Gr_A_horiz, 0)
"Nu_sphere_Churchill"
Nu_sphere_Churchill_A = Nu_sphere_Churchill(Pr_A_sphere, Gr_A_sphere)

"Case B - input set in the laminar range"
Pr_B = 0.7             "Prandtl number [-]"
Gr_B = 1E4             "Grashof number [-]"

"Nu_vertical_plate_Churchill"
Nu_vertical_plate_Churchill_B = Nu_vertical_plate_Churchill(Pr_B, Gr_B)
"Nu_horizontal_plate_McAdams"
Nu_horizontal_plate_McAdams_B = Nu_horizontal_plate_McAdams(Pr_B, Gr_B, 1)
Nu_horizontal_plate_McAdams_B_cold = Nu_horizontal_plate_McAdams(Pr_B, Gr_B, 0)
"Nu_horizontal_plate_VDI"
Nu_horizontal_plate_VDI_B = Nu_horizontal_plate_VDI(Pr_B, Gr_B, 1)
Nu_horizontal_plate_VDI_B_cold = Nu_horizontal_plate_VDI(Pr_B, Gr_B, 0)
"Nu_horizontal_plate_Rohsenow"
Nu_horizontal_plate_Rohsenow_B = Nu_horizontal_plate_Rohsenow(Pr_B, Gr_B, 1)
Nu_horizontal_plate_Rohsenow_B_cold = Nu_horizontal_plate_Rohsenow(Pr_B, Gr_B, 0)
"Nu_sphere_Churchill"
Nu_sphere_Churchill_B = Nu_sphere_Churchill(Pr_B, Gr_B)

"Case C - input set in the turbulent range"
Pr_C = 0.71            "Prandtl number [-]"
Gr_C = 2E10            "Grashof number [-]"

"Nu_vertical_plate_Churchill"
Nu_vertical_plate_Churchill_C = Nu_vertical_plate_Churchill(Pr_C, Gr_C)
"Nu_horizontal_plate_McAdams"
Nu_horizontal_plate_McAdams_C = Nu_horizontal_plate_McAdams(Pr_C, Gr_C, 1)
Nu_horizontal_plate_McAdams_C_cold = Nu_horizontal_plate_McAdams(Pr_C, Gr_C, 0)
"Nu_horizontal_plate_VDI"
Nu_horizontal_plate_VDI_C = Nu_horizontal_plate_VDI(Pr_C, Gr_C, 1)
Nu_horizontal_plate_VDI_C_cold = Nu_horizontal_plate_VDI(Pr_C, Gr_C, 0)
"Nu_horizontal_plate_Rohsenow"
Nu_horizontal_plate_Rohsenow_C = Nu_horizontal_plate_Rohsenow(Pr_C, Gr_C, 1)
Nu_horizontal_plate_Rohsenow_C_cold = Nu_horizontal_plate_Rohsenow(Pr_C, Gr_C, 0)
"Nu_sphere_Churchill"
Nu_sphere_Churchill_C = Nu_sphere_Churchill(Pr_C, Gr_C)
